{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-122768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/b7e72ce1ab38408f04f3b8d5a228ec0a3632c60086ef0a7abeb7e18af3002e6b.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dad7ec50e5da23662c4a7cfc1c1e9d19139830767c98df11b5fd70ce8739bca5"} {"question" "q-122768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/e41579712730d26c66d9f46f8d978fd51c8efbd76394c094113454e24b3aaff7.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $2$ and $4:$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/849f44d06de55f14811d433f9ccfdd30d47f6a1db1970cdfbfbd6f16be4959b0.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=2\\sqrt{5}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dad7ec50e5da23662c4a7cfc1c1e9d19139830767c98df11b5fd70ce8739bca5"} {"question" "q-122769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "5\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/69072873cb1f613ee9c2fa4a4caf3dc3409026c2a8310847b1c3dd890cc816d8.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $3$ and $6:$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/d0d9f0fad038f04b6d93920979161694f4e709a31b68083f20e768bf60aa1675.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=3\\sqrt{5}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dad7ec50e5da23662c4a7cfc1c1e9d19139830767c98df11b5fd70ce8739bca5"} {"question" "q-122770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "5\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\sqrt{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122766" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/1a46390ced6cc52c535f9f48e5e157cea83960caa339e98b23e3b64c93e0ea38.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122766" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is a line segment directed along the $y$-axis. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=3-(-1)=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122766" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dad7ec50e5da23662c4a7cfc1c1e9d19139830767c98df11b5fd70ce8739bca5"} {"question" "q-122766" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122766" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122766" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/d82f445e19af6e6d343bfab6c082108e945a8feb21caa9b60a2d93da24fc3f6a.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the line segment directed along the $x$-axis. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=5-1=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dad7ec50e5da23662c4a7cfc1c1e9d19139830767c98df11b5fd70ce8739bca5"} {"question" "q-122767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/d7844777c0bb540ba337756f45c4d71a8a8e9f9c84372e01542892801efecce3.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/98f444851e2df505d4242d1f8e4be0d76f78babd5d9633d9ab0ec84c245fe364.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is three-quarters of a circle of radius $r=2.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{3}{4}(2\\pi r)=3\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is a line segment of length $2.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=3\\pi +2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dad7ec50e5da23662c4a7cfc1c1e9d19139830767c98df11b5fd70ce8739bca5"} {"question" "q-122843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi +3"] ["math" "3\\pi +2"] ["math" "3\\pi +4"] ["math" "4\\pi +1"] ["math" "\\pi +2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\pi +2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/558dbbc424725ca0d9bad628968489cc2e32d67136102290666ff055a634bc16.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is a path that traverses a semicircle of radius $2$ once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/82fedadbb33b1747832bac25d9cda8e0b6b18b0fab411a870e90bc09ce47226e.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a semi-circle of radius $r=2.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\pi r=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is a line segment of length $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=2\\pi +4=2(\\pi +2).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dad7ec50e5da23662c4a7cfc1c1e9d19139830767c98df11b5fd70ce8739bca5"} {"question" "q-122481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2(2\\pi +1)"] ["math" "2(\\pi +1)"] ["math" "2(\\pi +2)"] ["math" "4(\\pi +1)"] ["math" "4(\\pi +2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2(\\pi +2)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/39a7cb0b31cb5d906803063597ae968be8fba90f048a4d9745225de7c3f8d6db.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The path $C$ is made up of three parts, as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/058c7b33112329df321e61660df4f2d388f42ca40c0aae5b73a089a0251726e2.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {C}_{3}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe paths ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are line segments of length $3.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\,\\text{d}s=3.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=3+5+3=11.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dad7ec50e5da23662c4a7cfc1c1e9d19139830767c98df11b5fd70ce8739bca5"} {"question" "q-122828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "11"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "11"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/bf87c4707c0f4a8ab1b5157a13e8c7785da915833c5f9d896a2747af27f70e59.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/ff52b8e83a9cc9867b25ea3c24ed0f988a27dec73f9e0ab2dfda28ddabb3238a.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above.\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $r=4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{1}{4}(2\\pi r)=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle where both legs have a length of $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=2\\pi +4\\sqrt{2}=2(\\pi +2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dad7ec50e5da23662c4a7cfc1c1e9d19139830767c98df11b5fd70ce8739bca5"} {"question" "q-122850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2(2\\pi +\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "2(\\pi +2\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "2\\pi +3\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi +4\\sqrt{2}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi +4\\sqrt{2}}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2(\\pi +2\\sqrt{2})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/68b3f78c29c9d7776efc2a78672ae53fe2e806d7ca6c3d663de8a16c03342132.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/27c19b56a24315f1105539afc441a33957dddf606b3410b86121e7cb8ed16973.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=5.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $2$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=2\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=5+2\\sqrt{5}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dad7ec50e5da23662c4a7cfc1c1e9d19139830767c98df11b5fd70ce8739bca5"} {"question" "q-122845" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122845" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "2(2+\\sqrt{5})"] ["math" "2(3+\\sqrt{5})"] ["math" "5+2\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122845" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5+2\\sqrt{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{3/2}⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 4.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3$\n- ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-3$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=12,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(u)=⟨4u,\\,8{u}^{3/2}⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\ny={x}^{3/2},\\,0\\le x\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dad7ec50e5da23662c4a7cfc1c1e9d19139830767c98df11b5fd70ce8739bca5"} {"question" "q-122816" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122816" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122816" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨2t,\\,2\\sqrt{t}⟩, & 0\\le t\\le 2 \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨4u,\\,2\\sqrt{2u}⟩, & 0\\le u\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=2k$\n- ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{2x},\\,0\\le x\\le 4.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dad7ec50e5da23662c4a7cfc1c1e9d19139830767c98df11b5fd70ce8739bca5"} {"question" "q-122868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(u)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{u}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{u}{2})⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 2\\pi .$ \n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=2,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le \\pi .$\n- ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-1$\n- ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we note that both parameterizations traverse a half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$ between $0$ and $\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dad7ec50e5da23662c4a7cfc1c1e9d19139830767c98df11b5fd70ce8739bca5"} {"question" "q-122815" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122815" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122815" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=⟨{t}^{4}-3{t}^{2},\\,t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 2.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-4$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=8,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(u)=⟨16{u}^{4}-12{u}^{2},\\,2u⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1.$\n- ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is false, while statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx={y}^{4}-3{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dad7ec50e5da23662c4a7cfc1c1e9d19139830767c98df11b5fd70ce8739bca5"} {"question" "q-122814" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122814" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122814" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+1},\\,3t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+1},\\,2u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-k$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{2k}{3}$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is false. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1},\\,0\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dad7ec50e5da23662c4a7cfc1c1e9d19139830767c98df11b5fd70ce8739bca5"} {"question" "q-122851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "q-122851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee23137851e8d53d273f1d52fc051b7c7c487c421f9cc257d8061b869ce60bfa"} {"question" "e-9470" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/765415456d7f33c3558e08e7c4fc01ef3e80961c1e073a210033d91741d78f60.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s,$ where $C$ is the arc of a semicircle of radius $4$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/example-9470.json" "file_sha256" "f3a884a54b87412edddae887e94f0e31c831e5b2fcc7e6fd6f2a0165e7a27a6a"} {"question" "e-9470" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $C$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 4.$ Its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=\\pi r=4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 4\\pi.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/example-9470.json" "file_sha256" "f3a884a54b87412edddae887e94f0e31c831e5b2fcc7e6fd6f2a0165e7a27a6a"} {"question" "e-9471" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/84de7754ed9244e3d646e452f6ed6dab349c130d17eae1d4dbc94cb52bd761e1.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above, traversed once counterclockwise." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/example-9471.json" "file_sha256" "39f1f57238eaba88410cfe1f975e183e2d525862d3e6c6686f0a892c3027478b"} {"question" "e-9471" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The path $C$ is made up of three parts, as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/f4e80a23910612bd55e7578a074f23c76535139889faff479c4dd0521e7496ff.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C = C_1 \\cup C_2 \\cup C_3$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s =  \\int_{C_1}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\int_{C_2}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\int_{C_3}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nThe path $C_1$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 3.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1} \\,\\textrm ds = \\dfrac{1}{2}(2 \\pi r) = 3\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe paths $C_2$ and $C_3$ are hypotenuses of right triangles whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} \\,\\textrm ds = \\int_{C_3} \\,\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 3\\pi + 5 + 5 = 3\\pi + 10.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/example-9471.json" "file_sha256" "39f1f57238eaba88410cfe1f975e183e2d525862d3e6c6686f0a892c3027478b"} {"question" "e-9472" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{3},\\,t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le 3 \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨3{u}^{3},\\,3u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = 3k$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/example-9472.json" "file_sha256" "93e2cccb3af62a668fd00953c06ea4c26a71b271fbe6835357fc7c51ac959de9"} {"question" "e-9472" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Notice that $C_1$ and $C_2$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that $C_1$ and $C_2$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{9}y^3, \\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds  = \\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/example-9472.json" "file_sha256" "93e2cccb3af62a668fd00953c06ea4c26a71b271fbe6835357fc7c51ac959de9"}] "usage" {"q-122768" [] "q-122481" [] "e-9472" [] "e-9471" [] "e-9470" []} "basis" 1845 "retractions" [[17592186167027 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3145/Source/Images/q-122768.png)\n\nCalculate $∫_{C}ds$, where $C$ is the line segment shown above."] [17592186167023 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3145/Source/Images/q-122481.png)\n\nCalculate $∫_{C}ds$, where $C$ is a path that traverses a semicircle of radius $2$ once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above."] [17592186166645 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s$, where $C$ is the arc of a semicircle of radius $4$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3145/Source/Images/238460fd6d89a120c21c88a681a7f2e3.png)"] [17592186166645 :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C$, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L\n$$\n\nIn this case, $C$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 4$. Its arc length is given by\n\n$$\nL = πr = 4π\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 4\\pi\n$$"] [17592186166646 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s$, where $C$ is the path shown above, traversed once counterclockwise.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3145/Source/Images/bb0d714825f714ebc1e0612dd9fc507e.png)"] [17592186166646 :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of three parts, as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3145/Source/Images/2dab4e2a2bea7b9217543ff7144f9dce.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C = C_1 \\cup C_2 \\cup C_3$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n$\\int_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s = \\int_{C_1}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\int_{C_2}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\int_{C_3}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s$.\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C$, i.e.,\n$\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L$.\n\nThe path $C_1$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 3$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1} \\,\\textrm ds = \\dfrac{1}{2}(2 \\pi r) = 3\\pi\n$$\n\nThe paths $C_2$ and $C_3$ are hypotenuses of right triangles whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} \\,\\textrm ds = \\int_{C_3} \\,\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 3\\pi + 5 + 5 = 3\\pi + 10\n$$"] [17592186166647 :question/problem "Consider the curves $C_1$ and $C_2$, defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}C_{1}:r_{1}(t)|= ⟨\\frac{1}{9}t^{3}, t⟩, & 0 \\le t \\le 3 \\\\ C_{2}:r_{2}(u)|= ⟨3u^{3}, 3u⟩, & 0 \\le u \\le 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow suppose $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k$. Which of the following must be true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = 3k$\n2. $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$\n3. $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$"] [17592186166647 :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Notice that $C_1$ and $C_2$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n$\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$.\nTo see that $C_1$ and $C_2$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n$x = \\dfrac{1}{9}y^3, \\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 3$.\n- Statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n$\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = \\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186163557 17592186163558 17592186163559 17592186163560 17592186163561 17592186163581 17592186163582 17592186163583 17592186163584 17592186163585] "immutable_fields" [[17592186163556 :answer-field/correct] [17592186163556 :answer-field/id] [17592186163556 :answer-field/key] [17592186163556 :answer-field/type] [17592186163580 :answer-field/correct] [17592186163580 :answer-field/id] [17592186163580 :answer-field/key] [17592186163580 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
